+\subsection{Case-control outcomes and sampling weights}
+\label{sec:weights-binary}
+
+HRATT also analyses binary outcomes (\code{trait="binary"}) and samples
+with sampling weights (\code{sample\_weights}), such as inverse
+probabilities of participation \citep{schoeler2023}, alone or together.
+Both enter one working model. Let $w$ be the sampling weights scaled to mean one and $v$
+the working weights: $v=w$ for a quantitative trait and
+$v=w\,\mu_0(1-\mu_0)$ for a binary one, where $\mu_0$ is the covariate-only
+(weighted) logistic fit. With $s=\sqrt v$, step~1 fits
+$\tilde\y=s\circ\y$ on $\tilde\X=s\circ\X$ and genotypes
+$\tilde\z_j=s\circ\z_j$, so every solver keeps its $v_g K+v_e\I$ form; the
+binary working response $(y-\mu_0)/\{\mu_0(1-\mu_0)\}$ makes this one
+iteratively reweighted least-squares step, as LDAK-KVIK's binary step~1 is a
+weighted linear regression \citep{hof2025}. Constant $v$ leaves the unscaled
+model unchanged, bit for bit.
+
+REML has no likelihood under sampling weights, so weighted fits use HE. The
+noise covariance of the scaled model is $SDS$ with $D=\diag(w)$, and the
+least-squares fit of $\tilde\y_s\tilde\y_s^\top$ on $v_gK+v_eSDS$ weights each
+diagonal entry by $1/D_{ii}$, so that every moment is design-consistent. With
+unit weights its normal equations are Equation~\eqref{eq:he-moments}. The
+structure test uses the Kish size of $v$ in place of $n$.
+
+Step~2 tests the score $U_j=\z_j^\top\mathbf a_g$ of a variant $j$ on
+chromosome group $g$, where $\mathbf a_g=s\circ\tilde{\mathbf r}_g$ is the
+weighted LOCO residual of a quantitative trait and
+$\mathbf a_g=w\circ(\y-\boldsymbol\mu_g)$ that of a binary one, $\boldsymbol\mu_g$
+being the weighted null logistic fit with the LOCO score as offset. Both are
+orthogonal to the covariates. When genotypes are exchangeable given the
+covariates, whatever the weights,
+\begin{equation}
+ \Var(U_j\mid\mathbf a_g)=\zeta_j\,\|\mathbf a_g\|^2,
+ \qquad \zeta_j=\frac{\z_j^\top\z_j-\|Q_X^\top\z_j\|^2}{n-p},
+ \label{eq:retro-variance}
+\end{equation}
+with $Q_X$ an orthonormal basis of the unscaled covariates. For an
+unweighted quantitative trait, $U_j^2/\{\zeta_j\|\mathbf a_g\|^2\}$ is the
+unweighted statistic. Above $|z|=2$, a saddlepoint approximation of $U_j$
+under the empirical distribution of the variant's genotype values (at most
+four, counting the no-call value), as in SPACox \citep{bi2020}, replaces the
+normal tail; $\lambda$ keeps its rule and multiplies the statistic.
+Quantitative effects are weighted least-squares slopes, binary ones
+one-step log odds ratios.
+
+Two common alternatives failed in pilots, and are not used. The
+Huber--White sandwich $\sum_i a_i^2 Z_{ij}^2$ of weighted association
+replaces each genotype's variance by its own square. Under selection on the
+outcome a few samples carry each score, and its test was inflated 17-fold
+at $\alpha=10^{-3}$ for minor allele frequency 1--5\%, as was LDAK's
+weighted linear regression. The model-based logistic variance
+$\sum_i\mu_i(1-\mu_i)\tilde g_{ij}^2$ of logistic score tests with
+saddlepoint tails \citep{dey2017,zhou2018,hof2025} trusts the fitted
+probabilities, which an in-sample LOCO offset overfits when cases are few:
+with HRATT's offsets and 50 cases among 5,000 it gave $\lgc=0.55$ on a null
+trait, against 1.04 for Equation~\eqref{eq:retro-variance}.
+
